== 1. THERMAL ENERGY MANAGEMENT: SKILLET AND GREASE FIRES
<thermal-energy-management-skillet-and-grease-fires>
The kitchen concentrates the densest thermal and chemical energies of the household. Knowing how to neutralize an emergency within three seconds marks the boundary between controlled composure and irreversible catastrophe.

#figure(image("/figures/livre_1/fig_securite_accidents_frigo_feu_en.svg", width: 95%, height: 36%, fit: "contain"),
  caption: [
    Kitchen Safety: The 4 Critical Emergencies and Life-Saving Reflexes.
  ]
)

=== A. Cooking Oil Fires (Pan Grease Fires)
<a.-cooking-oil-fires-pan-grease-fires>
When vegetable oil or animal fat exceeds its auto-ignition temperature (#strong[680°F / 360°C]), the oil film ignites spontaneously upon contacting atmospheric oxygen.
- #strong[The Deadly Mistake:] Pouring water onto burning oil. Liquid water is denser than hot oil ($d = 1.0 upright(" vs ") d = 0.9$). It plunges straight to the scorching metal bottom of the pan. In a fraction of a millisecond, it contacts the superheated metal and vaporizes violently: #strong[1 quart of liquid water produces 1,700 quarts of steam]! This explosive volumetric expansion atomizes the burning oil into a microscopic airborne aerosol that combusts instantly in room oxygen, generating a massive 10-foot (3-meter) fireball (*boilover*) that engulfs the ceiling, incinerates the room, and causes third-degree burns.
- #strong[The Only Life-Saving Countermeasure (in 3 Calm Gestures):]
  1. #emph[Kill the energy source:] Turn off the gas burner or switch off the induction/electric cooktop immediately.
  2. #emph[Smother the oxygen supply:] Slide a tight-fitting metal pan lid, baking sheet, or roasting tray horizontally across the rim of the pan. Deprived of oxygen ($upright("O")_2$), the fire suffocates within two seconds. If no metal lid is available, drape a heavy cloth towel #strong[soaked in water and vigorously wrung bone-dry] across the pan.
  3. #emph[Do not touch for 15 minutes:] Never lift the lid to check! The oil remains above 600°F (300°C): introducing a rush of fresh oxygen will trigger an immediate explosive backdraft. Never carry a flaming pan toward the sink.

=== B. Electrical and Appliance Fires
<b.-electrical-and-appliance-fires>
If a toaster, food processor, or oven sparks and smokes:
- Never use water (risk of fatal electrical shock via water conductivity).
- Unplug the wall outlet or trip the circuit breaker at the main distribution panel.
- Smother flames using a certified fire blanket or a Class B/C dry chemical / $upright("CO")_2$ extinguisher.

#divider()

== 2. THERMAL SCALDS AND BURNS: FROM FIRST REFLEX TO MEDICAL RESPONSE
<thermal-scalds-and-burns-from-first-reflex-to-medical-response>
=== A. The Medical Rule of the "Three 15s"
<a.-the-medical-rule-of-the-three-15s>
When scalding oil spatters or hot cast iron touches bare skin:
- #strong[15°C (60°F):] Cool running tap water (temperate, never ice-cold).
- #strong[15 Minutes:] Run water over the burn continuously for 15 full minutes without stopping.
- #strong[15 Centimeters (6 Inches):] Hold the burned area 15 cm beneath the tap to prevent mechanical water pressure from abrading fragile epidermal tissue.

#oeil_molecule[
==== 🔬 Why Cool for a Full 15 Minutes?
<why-cool-for-a-full-15-minutes>
Human skin is a poor thermal conductor: after a burn, residual heat remains trapped within deep dermal layers, continuing to cook and destroy cellular proteins beneath the surface for minutes after contact ends. Running temperate water for 15 minutes dissipates this deep heat reservoir by continuous convective extraction, halting the burn before it penetrates into third-degree nerve layers.
]

=== B. Two Fatal Myths to Ban Forever
<b.-two-fatal-myths-to-ban-forever>
- #strong[Never apply butter, oil, or toothpaste to a fresh burn:] Lipids form an insulating thermal blanket that traps heat deep inside the flesh, accelerating tissue necrosis.
- #strong[Never apply ice cubes:] Extreme sub-zero contact constricts capillaries, causing frostbite and ischemic tissue death atop the thermal burn.

#divider()

== 3. KNIFE CUTS, STEEL BIOMECHANICS, AND EMERGENCY HEMOSTASIS
<knife-cuts-steel-biomechanics-and-emergency-hemostasis>
#figure(image("/figures/livre_1/fig_posture_griffe_de_chat_en.svg", width: 95%, height: 36%, fit: "contain"),
  caption: [
    Knife ergonomics: Pinch grip and the cat's claw guiding fence.
